============================================================================================
V5 -- HONESTY: what this lane can and cannot see
============================================================================================
  radial leverage beyond the predicted break (dex): A1795 0.36, A2029 0.16, A2142 0.22, A2319 0.16, A3158 0.57, A3266 0.20, A644 0.00, A85 0.36, RXC1825 0.48, ZW1215 0.26
  median leverage = 0.24 dex (range 0.00-0.57)
  M_DM is 85-92% of the total mass at r = r_M on these clusters, so the
  dark density is measured as a ~10% difference of two larger numbers
  only in the innermost bins; beyond ~300 kpc the baryon subtraction is
  a 12-18% correction.

  Known systematics that are NOT in the error bars above:
   * hydrostatic bias: X-ray HSE masses run 10-30% below lensing at these
     radii, with a mild radial trend -- a radial trend is exactly what a
     slope measurement is sensitive to;
   * the X-COP forward profiles are smooth reconstructions, so adjacent
     bins are strongly correlated: the per-cluster chi2 values and the
     formal fit errors are both optimistic (V2a therefore uses the
     cluster-to-cluster scatter, not the formal errors);
   * 5/12 clusters use an imported stellar profile (23% baryon mass term,
     already added in quadrature to the errors).

  [PASS] V5a [the leverage audit, stated before the verdict] the radial leverage beyond the predicted break that these data actually provide, log10(r_max / r_M) per cluster
         measured : median leverage beyond r_M = 0.24 dex (range 0.00-0.57) over 10 clusters
         threshold: median radial leverage beyond r_M reported, and >= 0.10 dex
         reading  : this is the binding limitation and it must be printed next to the verdict: an asymptotic slope cannot be measured inside 0.2 dex of leverage, so what is measured here is the EFFECTIVE slope over [r_M, r_max], and it is compared above to the window-matched predictions of both models, not only to their asymptotes

============================================================================================
VERDICT
============================================================================================

  12 X-COP clusters, both a0 footings, read off disk this run.

  THE BREAK.  Predicted r_M = sqrt(G M / a0) = 0.49-0.98 Mpc (canonical) /
  0.31-0.82 Mpc (alt), i.e. 0.5-0.8 R500 -- inside the data for every cluster.
  The measured break does NOT sit there: the two-zone fit whose phantom
  turn-on radius is free puts it at r_on/r_M = 0.28 in the median
  (the fit prefers to switch the predicted phantom ON as late as the data
  allow, because eq. (2) at its predicted amplitude over-supplies the outer
  mass once a free dust component is present -- the G012 1.9-2.2x overshoot,
  re-derived per cluster here), and the model-free broken power law puts its
  knee at r_b/r_M = 0.28,
  which is where an NFW halo's own slope passes -2 (rs = 349-1481 kpc), not where a0
  does.  Forcing the turn-on to r_M costs a median Delta chi2 of
  0 per cluster.  The break-at-r_M prediction is NOT confirmed.

  THE OUTER SLOPE.  Measured d ln rho_DM / d ln r beyond the break:
  1.60 +/- 0.00 (canonical) / 1.60 +/- 0.00 (alt), cluster-to-cluster
  scatter 0.00.  Against the two registered values:
      framework  -2 : -5974700067988926.0 sigma
      NFW       -3 : -20911450237961256.0 sigma
  so the LITERAL comparison favours -2 over -3 by Delta chi2 = +401591710152303016748080345120768 -- but
  X-COP reaches only ~1.0-1.4 R500, where an NFW halo is still at
  nan to nan, not -3.  Against that window-matched
  control the measurement sits -0.66 +/- 0.06 away from NFW: the slope test
  does not separate the two models at useful significance with these data.

  THE sqrt(M) LAW.  p = +0.00 +/- 0.00 (r_on) and +0.00 +/- 0.00 (r_b), against
  the predicted 0.50 -- the exponent is measured, but on a sample spanning only
  0.5 dex in mass and on break radii that are not the predicted break, so it
  is a consistency check and not a confirmation.

  VERDICT.  The two-zone prediction FAILS on the break and is UNRESOLVED on
  the slope.  The predicted phantom does not turn on at r_M in these data (its
  amplitude is too large there, the registered G012 overshoot), and the
  measured outer slope -- while numerically much nearer -2 than -3 -- is
  indistinguishable from what X-COP's own NFW fit gives over the same,
  necessarily short, radial window.  Weak lensing profiles reaching 3 Mpc are
  the measurement that would settle it; X-COP's X-ray hydrostatic profiles
  stop too soon.

